Package: ocean-mist
Version: @VERSION@
Section: sound
Priority: optional
Architecture: all
Maintainer: NahuelGenchi <65604505+NahuelGenchi@users.noreply.github.com>
Installed-Size: @INSTALLED_SIZE@
Depends: python3 (>= 3.11), python3-gi, gir1.2-gtk-3.0, gir1.2-webkit2-4.1
Breaks: apparmor (<< 4.0~)
Recommends: gstreamer1.0-plugins-good, gstreamer1.0-plugins-bad, gstreamer1.0-libav
Suggests: firefox | firefox-esr
Homepage: https://github.com/NahuelGenchi/ocean-mist
Description: music player with the look of Windows Media Player 9
 Ocean Mist plays your music files and internet radio in a window drawn like
 the classic Windows Media Player 9: the sculpted frame, the visualizations, a
 ten-band equalizer, a media library and playlists. It also searches and plays
 YouTube Music, and shows and plays your Apple Music library, once you have
 signed in through Firefox, which this package only suggests. It is an
 unofficial fan project, not affiliated with Microsoft.
 .
 Apple Music songs play in a playback engine that the player downloads when
 you ask it to, after showing what it downloads. On Ubuntu 24.04 and later
 that engine needs a permission for its security sandbox, and this package
 installs it as an AppArmor profile, /etc/apparmor.d/ocean-mist-engine, so the
 player has nothing to ask you for. The profile names one file, the engine's
 runtime in ~/.local/share/ocean-mist/engine in each user's home folder, and
 gives it one thing: creating user namespaces. As that file is in a folder its
 user can write, a program that anything running as that user puts there gets
 the same permission. "sudo aa-disable ocean-mist-engine" switches it off.
 .
 The profile needs AppArmor 4.0 or later, so this package cannot be installed
 next to an older one (Debian 12, for example).
